    "['set policy-options policy-statement [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'route-map ospf-mpls-te permit 10', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'route-map ospf-mpls-te permit 10', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco configuration for the Juniper policy statement:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map ospf-mpls-te permit 10\\n match protocol ospf\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Juniper `policy-statement` translates to Cisco's `route-map`\\n- `term 1` becomes the sequence number `10` (Cisco typically uses 10, 20, etc.)\\n- `from protocol ospf` becomes `match protocol ospf`\\n- `then accept` is implied by `permit` in the route-map statement\\n- The second Juniper line isn't needed as a separate command in Cisco since the action is included in the route-map declaration\", 'trans_template': 'route-map ospf-mpls-te permit 10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'match protocol ospf', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'match protocol ospf', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco configuration for the Juniper policy statement:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map ospf-mpls-te permit 10\\n match protocol ospf\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Juniper `policy-statement` translates to Cisco's `route-map`\\n- `term 1` becomes the sequence number `10` (Cisco typically uses 10, 20, etc.)\\n- `from protocol ospf` becomes `match protocol ospf`\\n- `then accept` is implied by `permit` in the route-map statement\\n- The second Juniper line isn't needed as a separate command in Cisco since the action is included in the route-map declaration\", 'trans_template': 'match protocol ospf'}]]": 1,
