    "['set routing-options forwarding-table export [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router ospf 1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'router ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n  redistribute ospf 1 subnets\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Cisco, there isn\\'t a direct 1:1 equivalent for Juniper\\'s \"forwarding-table export\" command. The closest concept is route redistribution.\\n2. The above assumes you want to redistribute OSPF routes into another OSPF process (common in MPLS TE scenarios). If you need to redistribute into another protocol, the command would differ.\\n3. The \"subnets\" keyword is added to ensure all subnet routes are redistributed, similar to Juniper\\'s default behavior.', 'trans_template': 'router ospf 1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'redistribute ospf 1 subnets', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'redistribute ospf 1 subnets', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n  redistribute ospf 1 subnets\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Cisco, there isn\\'t a direct 1:1 equivalent for Juniper\\'s \"forwarding-table export\" command. The closest concept is route redistribution.\\n2. The above assumes you want to redistribute OSPF routes into another OSPF process (common in MPLS TE scenarios). If you need to redistribute into another protocol, the command would differ.\\n3. The \"subnets\" keyword is added to ensure all subnet routes are redistributed, similar to Juniper\\'s default behavior.', 'trans_template': 'redistribute ospf 1 subnets'}]]": 1
